licated tech, quick decisions. D problem solving, concentration.\n\nCalmness, judgment under pressure: B handles stress well. C keeps calm in crisis, quick decisions. D responds adequately to unexpected events. A anticipate dangerous situations maybe good.\n\nDecision-making: C quick correct decisions. D solves problems extremely well. A sees complex connections maybe good.\n\nTeamwork/communication: A leadership but show-off, not open to new ideas may hinder teamwork. B makes nasty remarks about colleagues - bad teamwork. C difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric - poor teamwork. D know-it-all, hot temper - may affect crew cooperation.\n\nProfessional development/long-term suitability: B conscientious, but weak memory for numbers maybe training needed. D hot temper may be issue. A not open to new ideas may hinder development. C not willing to further education.\n\nOverall, which candidate best balances? Need to weigh seriousness for safe long-distance flights. Teamwork and communication crucial. A leadership but show-off and not open to ideas could be problematic. B conscientious but nasty remarks - crew cooperation suffers. C difficulty communicating, egocentric - likely poor. D hot temper and know-it-all - also problematic.\n\nAll have issues. Which is least problematic? Perhaps Candidate D: strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, handles unexpected events. Negatives: hot temper, know-it-all. Could affect crew but maybe manageable with training. B's nasty remarks directly affect crew morale. A's show-off and closed to ideas also problematic. C's communication issues severe.\n\nThus D might be best despite temper.\n\nWe should state provisional preference D, with confidence moderate, note uncertainties about temperament impact, ask others about any additional teamwork evidence.\n\nWe need to produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE summarizing.\n\nProceed."}
