ve stronger technical/responsibility profiles (D) or better crew morale (C) but carry more severe communication or temperament deficits.\n**Remaining uncertainty**\n- How much A\u2019s reluctance to accept criticism and new ideas will actually affect day\u2011to\u2011day crew interaction and adaptability on long routes.\n- Whether A\u2019s organizational lapses can be mitigated through support staff or procedural checks.\n**What would change my vote**\n- Concrete evidence that A\u2019s leadership translates into effective, open crew communication despite the \u201cshow\u2011off\u201d perception.\n- Evidence that Candidate\u202fD\u2019s hot temper and know\u2011it\u2011all attitude are well\u2011controlled in prolonged, high\u2011stress situations, making his operational strengths outweigh the interpersonal risks.\n- Strong positive teamwork/communication data for Candidate\u202fC that outweigh his communication difficulties, or similar data for Candidate\u202fB that mitigate their interpersonal negatives.\nMETADATA_JSON:\n{\"agent\": \"agent_1\", \"vote\": \"Candidate A\"}", "thoughts": "We need to produce public message and metadata as agent_1. We have to follow the system instruction: we are agent 1, need to give our current preferred candidate, summarize evidence, etc. We have already contributed earlier (Agent 1 gave teamwork info). Now we need to give our own provisional preference. We have the shared mental model and other agents' positions: agent_2 votes D, agent_3 votes C. We need to evaluate and give our own.\n\nWe have all candidate info. Let's evaluate.\n\nCandidate A: strengths: anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Weaknesses: show-off, not open to new ideas, not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized. Teamwork: leadership but show-off may hinder crew interaction.\n\nCandidate B: strengths: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent com